<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Understanding exponetial and natural logarithms</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the following questions:</p>
<ol type="a">
    <li>
        <p>How is the constant \(e\) defined? Select all that apply</p>
        <p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p>
        <p>[[feedback:prt1]]</p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>What is the approximate value of \(e\) to \(5\) decimalplaces?</p>
        <p>\(\quad e \approx \) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>
        <p>[[feedback:prt2]]</p>
    </li>

    <li>
        <p>What is the natural logarithmic function?</p>
        <p>[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]]</p>
        <p>[[feedback:prt3]]</p>
    </li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked solution</h3>
<ol type="a">
        <li>
<p>How is the constant e defined in terms of the exponential function?</p>
<p>The number \(e\) is the limit \(\lim_{n \to \infty}\left(\displaystyle 1 + \frac{1}{n}\right)^{n},\) an expression arises in the computation of compound interest, which is the sum of iifinite series:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle e= \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} 1 + \frac{1}{1!} + \frac{1}{2!} + \frac{1}{3!} + \cdots\)</p>
    </li>

        <li>
<p>What is the approximate value of \(e\) to \(5\) decimalplaces?</p>
<p>The approximate value of the mathematical constant \(e\) is approximately \({@float(%e)@}\), to \(5\)dp \(e \approx {@ta_2@}\).
It's an irrational number and it's transcendental, meaning it cannot be expressed as the solution to any polynomial equation with rational coefficients.</p>
  </li>

        <li>
<p>What is the natural logarithmic function?</p>
<p>The natural logarithmic function, denoted as \(\log_{e}(x)=\ln(x)\), is the logarithm to the base \(e\).</p>
<p>For example:
\[\begin{align}
\ln(e) &amp;= 1 \quad \text{(since } e^1 = e\text{)} \\
\ln(1) &amp;= 0 \quad \text{(since } e^0 = 1\text{)} \\
\ln(e^2) &amp;= 2 \quad \text{(since } e^2 = e^2\text{)}
\end{align}\]</p>
    </li>
        </ol>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
